<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">San Lorenzo Tenochtitlán</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1400–900 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century-bce">15th century BCE</a> · Also known as San Lorenzo Tenochtitlan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">San Lorenzo Tenochtitlán is the collective name for three related Olmec archaeological sites in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>: San Lorenzo, Tenochtitlán and Potrero Nuevo. San Lorenzo, on a plateau 50 metres above the Coatzacoalcos floodplain, was the major Olmec centre from about 1400 to 900 BCE and, from about 1200 BCE, the largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>. Its people reshaped the plateau with vast earth fills and stone drains, and ten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads">colossal heads</a> have been found there. Matthew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stirling">Stirling</a>, the first to excavate there, found colossal heads in 1945 and 1946.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-lorenzo-tenochtitlan.jpg" alt="San Lorenzo Tenochtitlán" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Madman2001 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CabezaColosal1_MuseoXalapa_v1.1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
